   "stratum": "chat",
   "source": "lmsys",
   "prompt": "Which of these senteces are the best: \"flexibility as far as the goals are achieved regularly\" or \"flexibility as far as the goals are regularly achieved\"",
   "response": "The second sentence is slightly better:\n\n**\"flexibility as far as the goals are regularly achieved\"**\n\nHere's why:\n\n- Both sentences are grammatically correct and mean the same thing.\n- However, in English, it is generally more natural to place the adverb (**regularly**) **before the main verb** (\"are achieved\") rather than at the end of the clause, especially in formal or written contexts.\n- \"Regularly achieved\" flows more smoothly than \"achieved regularly\" in this structure.\n\nThat said, the phrase \"as far as\" is a bit unusual here. If you mean \"provided that\" or \"on the condition that,\" you might consider:\n\n> **\"flexibility as long as the goals are regularly achieved\"**\n\nor\n\n> **\"flexibility provided that the goals are regularly achieved\"**\n\nBut between your two options, **\"flexibility as far as the goals are regularly achieved\"** is the better choice.",
