   "prompt": "Could you show me some examples of types in both haskell and rust?",
   "response": "I'll show you equivalent type concepts in Haskell and Rust, with comments explaining the similarities and differences.\n\n## 1. **Basic Types**\n\n```haskell\n-- Haskell: Simple types\nx :: Int\nx = 42\n\ny :: Double\ny = 3.14\n\nz :: Bool\nz = True\n\nname :: String\nname = \"Hello\"\n```\n\n```rust\n// Rust: Simple types\nlet x: i32 = 42;        // 32-bit integer\nlet y: f64 = 3.14;      // 64-bit float\nlet z: bool = true;     // boolean\nlet name: String = String::from(\"Hello\");\n// or: let name = \"Hello\"; // &str type\n```\n\n## 2. **Function Types**\n\n```haskell\n-- Haskell: Function types\nadd :: Int -> Int -> Int\nadd x y = x + y\n\n-- Higher-order function\napply :: (Int -> Int) -> Int -> Int\napply f x = f x\n```\n\n```rust\n// Rust: Function types (using closures)\nlet add = |x: i32, y: i32| -> i32 { x + y };\n\n// Higher-order function\nfn apply(f: impl Fn(i32) -> i32, x: i32) -> i32 {\n    f(x)\n}\n// Or with explicit function pointer:\nfn apply_ptr(f: fn(i32) -> i32, x: i32) -> i32 {\n    f(x)\n}\n```\n\n## 3. **Tuples**\n\n```haskell\n-- Haskell: Tuples\npair :: (Int, String)\npair = (42, \"answer\")\n\ntriple :: (Int, Float, Char)\ntriple = (1, 2.0, 'c')\n\n-- Access with pattern matching\nfirst :: (a, b) -> a\nfirst (x, _) = x\n```\n\n```rust\n// Rust: Tuples\nlet pair: (i32, &str) = (42, \"answer\");\n\nlet triple: (i32, f64, char) = (1, 2.0, 'c');\n\n// Access with destructuring\nfn first<T, U>((x, _): (T, U)) -> T {\n    x\n}\n// Or: pair.0\n```\n\n## 4. **Algebraic Data Types**\n\n### Product Types (Structs)\n\n```haskell\n-- Haskell: Product",
