   "prompt": "How many snowflakes fall during a typical winter? Try to explain your answer. Your explanation should take the reader through your reasoning step-by-step.",
   "response": "Let’s walk through this step-by-step.\n\n---\n\n**Step 1: Define scope**\n\nFirst, we need to decide what counts as “a typical winter” and “a typical place.”  \nSince there’s huge variation—a winter in Miami vs. one in northern Canada—we’ll consider a mid-sized northern hemisphere location, something like the northern United States or central Canada, over the entire winter season (roughly December through February).\n\nWe’ll also consider “snowflakes” to mean individual ice crystals or small aggregates that fall separately (recognizable as flakes).\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Estimate average snowfall depth**\n\nIn many northern U.S. states (e.g., Michigan, New York, Minnesota), total seasonal snowfall is around 60 inches (150 cm). Sometimes more, sometimes less, but 60 inches is a reasonable reference.\n\nWe’ll convert that depth into snow volume. Over one square foot (a 12 × 12 inch area), 60 inches of snow gives:\n\n\\[\n60 \\text{ inches of snow per square foot} = 60 \\text{ cubic inches of snow?}\n\\]\n\nNot exactly—snowpack compresses under its own weight. Fresh snow is much fluffier.  \nTo avoid worrying about compaction, let’s imagine we count flakes *as they fall*, so we use fresh snow density.\n\n---\n\n**Step 3: Determine the number of flakes per volume**\n\nA typical small snowflake might be about 1–2 mm in diameter. Let’s go with an **average flake volume** of roughly \\( 2 \\text{ mm}^3\\), remembering that snowflakes are mostly air.\n\nFresh snow density is about 0.1 g/cm³ (water is 1 g/cm³).  \nIf a flake’s solid ice mass is maybe 0.02 mg, then in one cubic centimeter of fresh snow, there might be around:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{0.1 \\text{ g/cm}^3}{0.00002 \\text{ g/flake}} \\approx 5,000 \\text{ flakes per cm}^3\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Step 4: Convert to inches and area**\n\nOne cubic inch equals about \\( 16.4 \\text{ cm}^3\\).\n\nSo one cubic inch of snow contains roughly:\n\n\\[\n5,000 \\times 16.4 \\approx 82,000 \\text{ flakes}\n\\]\n\nNow, how much snow volume falls per square foot in a season?\n\n",
