   "response": "Sorting a pandas DataFrame by a column is straightforward using the `sort_values()` method. Below are comprehensive examples with code and expected outputs.\n\n## Basic Sorting by a Single Column\n\n```python\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Sample DataFrame\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'Name': ['Alice', 'Bob', 'Charlie', 'David'],\n    'Age': [25, 30, 22, 35],\n    'Salary': [50000, 60000, 55000, 65000]\n})\nprint(\"Original DataFrame:\")\nprint(df)\n```\n\n**Output:**\n```\n      Name  Age  Salary\n0    Alice   25   50000\n1      Bob   30   60000\n2  Charlie   22   55000\n3    David   35   65000\n```\n\n### Ascending order (default):\n\n```python\ndf_sorted = df.sort_values(by='Age')\nprint(\"\\nSorted by Age (ascending):\")\nprint(df_sorted)\n```\n\n**Output:**\n```\n      Name  Age  Salary\n2  Charlie   22   55000\n0    Alice   25   50000\n1      Bob   30   60000\n3    David   35   65000\n```\n\n### Descending order:\n\n```python\ndf_sorted_desc = df.sort_values(by='Age', ascending=False)\nprint(\"\\nSorted by Age (descending):\")\nprint(df_sorted_desc)\n```\n\n**Output:**\n```\n      Name  Age  Salary\n3    David   35   65000\n1      Bob   30   60000\n0    Alice   25   50000\n2  Charlie   22   55000\n```\n\n## Sorting by Multiple Columns\n\n```python\ndf_multi = df.sort_values(by=['Age', 'Salary'], ascending=[True, False])\nprint(\"\\nSorted by Age ascending, then Salary descending:\")\nprint(df_multi)\n```\n\n**Output:**\n```\n      Name  Age  Salary\n2  Charlie   22   55000\n0    Alice   25   50000\n1      Bob   30   60000\n3    David   35   65000\n```\n(Age is primary sort key, then Salary in descending order; since only one row per age, the second key has no visible effect here.)\n\n## In-Place Sorting (modifying original DataFrame)\n\n```python\ndf.sort_values(by='Age', inplace=True)\nprint(\"\\nAfter in-place sort:\")\nprint(df)\n```\n\n**Note:** Using `",
