   "id": "chat-lmsys-0051",
   "stratum": "chat",
   "source": "lmsys",
   "prompt": "What are 10 common misconceptions about psychedelic therapies?",
   "response": "Here are 10 common misconceptions about psychedelic therapies, clarified with factual context:\n\n1. **“They get you ‘high’ in a recreational sense.”**  \n   *Misconception:* Psychedelic therapy is just about having a drug-induced euphoria.  \n   *Reality:* Therapeutic sessions are carefully controlled, with the goal of promoting introspective, often challenging psychological breakthroughs—not recreation.\n\n2. **“They’re a quick fix or magic cure.”**  \n   *Misconception:* One or two sessions will permanently resolve deep mental health issues.  \n   *Reality:* They are a tool within a broader therapeutic process, requiring integration sessions, preparation, and ongoing work.\n\n3. **“They’re only for severe, treatment-resistant depression.”**  \n   *Misconception:* These therapies are last-resort options only.  \n   *Reality:* Research is exploring their use for a range of conditions including anxiety, PTSD, addiction, and end-of-life distress, not just severe cases.\n\n4. **“They’re all the same – just different illegal drugs.”**  \n   *Misconception:* Psilocybin, MDMA, LSD, and ketamine all work identically.  \n   *Reality:* Each has unique pharmacological mechanisms (e.g., MDMA primarily enhances emotional openness; psilocybin alters perception and narrative; ketamine blocks glutamate receptors) and specific therapeutic protocols.\n\n5. **“They bypass the need for therapy.”**  \n   *Misconception:* The drug itself does all the work.  \n   *Reality:* The therapeutic setting, preparation, and especially post-session integration are equally critical for sustained benefits.\n\n6. **“They always cause mystical awe and positive breakthroughs.”**  \n   *Misconception:* Psychedelic experiences are uniformly pleasant.  \n   *Reality:* They can involve intense fear, confusion, or reliving trauma (“bad trips”), which trained therapists help navigate and integrate.\n\n7. **“They’re proven safe for everyone, including people with psychosis risk.”**  \n   *Misconception:* Anyone can try them.  \n   *Reality:* Screening excludes those with personal or family history of psychotic disorders, bipolar disorder, or other contraindications due to potential for triggering psychosis.\n\n8. **“They’re just ‘relegalized’ drugs from the 1960s.”**  \n   *Misconception:* Modern therapies are identical to recreational use in the past.  \n   *Reality:* Today",
