   "source": "dailydialog",
   "prompt": "Are there many idioms in English?",
   "response": "Yes, there are **many idioms in English**—thousands, in fact. They are a common and colorful part of the language, used in both casual conversation and formal writing.\n\nHere are a few key points to give you a sense of their prevalence:\n\n* **Estimated Number:** While no one has an exact count, most comprehensive dictionaries list several thousand idiomatic expressions. Many language experts estimate there are at least **10,000 to 25,000 idioms** that are in active or recognized use.\n* **Natural Part of Speech:** Native English speakers use idioms constantly, often without realizing it. Think of phrases like \"on the other hand,\" \"by the way,\" \"all of a sudden,\" or \"break a leg.\" These are so embedded in everyday language that they are unavoidable.\n* **The Challenge for Learners:** For non-native speakers, idioms are one of the most challenging aspects of English. Because their meanings are not literal, you can't guess the meaning from the individual words. For example, \"to spill the beans\" doesn't involve actual beans; it means \"to reveal a secret.\" This is why idioms are a major focus of advanced language learning.\n* **Categories of Idioms:** They cover all topics and emotions:\n    * **Happiness:** \"On cloud nine,\" \"over the moon,\" \"tickled pink\"\n    * **Anger:** \"See red,\" \"blow a fuse,\" \"hot under the collar\"\n    * **Time:** \"In the nick of time,\" \"once in a blue moon,\" \"call it a day\"\n    * **Business/Work:** \"Think outside the box,\" \"cut corners,\" \"get the ball rolling\"\n    * **Weather & Nature:** \"Raining cats and dogs,\" \"under the weather,\" \"a storm in a teacup\"\n\nSo, yes, there is a huge and vibrant collection of idioms in English. They are a defining feature of the language and essential for understanding and sounding natural.",
