  {
   "id": "chat-dailydialog-0007",
   "stratum": "chat",
   "source": "dailydialog",
   "prompt": "I was scared stiff of giving my first performance.",
   "response": "It's completely normal to feel terrified before a first performance. That fear means you care deeply about doing well, and it shows that you're stepping out of your comfort zone—which is where growth happens.\n\nHere are a few thoughts to help you through it:\n\n1. **The audience is on your side.** They’re not there to judge you harshly; they want to see you succeed and enjoy the experience. Most people have felt stage fright themselves, so they'll empathize.\n\n2. **Focus on the message, not yourself.** When you shift your attention from \"How am I looking?\" to \"What do I want to share?\" the nerves often quiet down.\n\n3. **Turn the fear into fuel.** Nervousness and excitement feel almost identical in the body. Instead of saying \"I'm scared,\" try saying \"I'm excited and ready.\" It reframes the experience.\n\n4. **Accept imperfection.** Even seasoned performers stumble or miss a note. What matters is how you recover and keep going. The audience will often not even notice small slip-ups.\n\nYou’ve already been brave just by signing up. Once you start, the hardest part is over—and you'll likely find a burst of energy that carries you through. And after? The relief and pride will make it all worth it.\n\nYou’ve got this. One step at a time.",
